<!-- ========================================================================
     1. SENIOR CAPSTONE DESIGN (2025–2026)
     ======================================================================== -->
<article class="treatise-section" id="capstone">
  <div class="treatise-header">
    <h2 class="treatise-title">Senior Capstone: P42 Robotic Planetary Sample Acquisition System</h2>
    <div class="treatise-meta">
      Rose-Hulman Institute of Technology &middot; Sangelkar Mechanical Engineering Laboratory (AY 2025&ndash;2026)<br>
      <strong>Role:</strong> Project Manager &amp; Lead Mechanical Designer &middot; <strong>Advisors:</strong> Dr. Sangelkar, Dr. Bowman, Dr. Vitral
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="lead">
    Securing mineral and core samples in extraterrestrial or deep subsea environments presents an acute kinematic paradox: under fractional gravity or neutral buoyancy, applying downward cutting force or rotational torque to bedrock pushes the robotic vehicle directly away from the terrain. In the P42 Capstone project, our four-engineer team engineered a self-anchoring, autonomous core extraction and sample carousel system designed to harvest geological samples from jagged, unconstrained rock faces.
  </p>

  <div class="metric-strip">
    <div class="metric-item">
      <div class="metric-value">&gt; 180 N</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Passive Anchor Shear Force</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-item">
      <div class="metric-value">4-Slot</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Geneva Indexing Carousel</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-item">
      <div class="metric-value">15&ndash;25 N</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Closed-Loop Plunge Force</div>
    </div>
    <div class="metric-item">
      <div class="metric-value">0.0 N&middot;m</div>
      <div class="metric-label">Net Reaction Torque (Oscillatory)</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Key Subsystems &amp; Mechanical Architecture</h3>

  <h4>1. JPL-Inspired Differential Microspine Gripper Array</h4>
  <p>
    Rather than requiring massive hydraulic or electrical clamping mechanisms, the anchor pads utilize an array of hardened steel microspines mounted on independent compliant four-bar linkages. Inspired by planetary sampling research at NASA JPL, our mechanism introduces a multi-tier closed-loop cable differential (whiffletree):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Continuous Tendon Differential:</strong> A continuous UHMWPE synthetic tendon routes through Delrin and oil-embedded bronze sleeve pulleys, allowing spines that hit rock asperities to stop while the tendon redistributes equalized tension across the remaining floating spines.</li>
    <li><strong>Passive Linkage Return:</strong> Constant force springs and surgical elastomeric tubing provide restorative compliance, ensuring immediate reset when tension relaxes without requiring active bilateral cabling.</li>
    <li><strong>Shear Adhesion:</strong> Tested to sustain over 180 Newtons of lateral and normal shear loading on cast concrete test substrates before microspine pull-out.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>2. High-Frequency Oscillating Core Toolhead</h4>
  <p>
    Conventional rotary drills generate continuous reactive torsion that twists rovers off their anchor footholds. To eliminate rotational reaction moments, I reverse-engineered a high-frequency oscillatory toolhead:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Zero-Reaction Kinematics:</strong> By reciprocating back and forth through a minute arc (~3.2&deg;) at 18,000 oscillations per minute, net reaction torque remains virtually zero.</li>
    <li><strong>TPU Bellows Environmental Boot:</strong> Designed and additive-manufactured flexible 3D printed TPU sealing bellows to isolate internal needle bearings and bevel gearsets from abrasive slurry and crushed basalt dust.</li>
    <li><strong>Inline Thrust Load Cell:</strong> Integrated an axial compression load cell directly behind the linear lead-screw drive carriage. A real-time closed-loop feed algorithm modulates plunge velocity to maintain cutter contact force within a 15&ndash;25 N window, optimizing specific cutting energy while safeguarding against anchor fracture.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4>3. Geneva Mechanism Multi-Station Core Carousel</h4>
  <p>
    For multi-site planetary sampling, storing multiple extracted cores without risking jamming is paramount. I designed an indexing sample turret actuated by a precision four-slot Geneva drive powered by a NEMA stepper motor:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Deterministic Mechanical Dwell:</strong> The Geneva drive provides positive mechanical locking at every station, guaranteeing that sample vials align perfectly with the transfer basket regardless of sensor drift or extreme temperature fluctuations.</li>
    <li><strong>Lightweight Sheet Metal Architecture:</strong> Evaluated aerospace aluminum versus stainless steel, optimizing bend reliefs and flange stiffness for rapid fabrication and structural rigidity.</li>
  </ul>

